# Recompile the C library with the tcc that has just been built.
#
# Each round of tcc links against the library the round below it compiled, so
# every round rebuilds it: the same sources, compiled by a compiler that
# understands more of C than the one before.  For the first round that
# compiler is tcc rather than MesCC, which is the point -- these are the
# gcc-variant sources, the fuller set MesCC could not manage.
#
# `tcc -ar` is given no flag cluster.  Mainline tcc scans every argument for
# the operations it refuses -- habdiopN -- and "-ar" itself contains an 'a',
# so `-ar cr lib.a obj` is rejected as a conflicting operation and prints its
# usage.  With no cluster it creates the archive, which is what `cr` asked
# for; the fork's tcc accepts that form too, so both rounds use it.
#
# Every flag is written out rather than expanded from one variable: kaem
# substitutes a variable as a SINGLE argument, spaces and all, so a variable
# holding "-I /a -I /b" arrives as one unreadable option rather than four.
# Anything that has to be several arguments is several variables.
#
# The include paths are the ones MesCC was given, and ONLY those: Mes's
# headers, and the directory holding what ./configure would have generated --
# mes/config.h and the arch/ headers that lib/linux/*.c reaches for as
# <arch/syscall.h>.
#
# tcc's own headers are deliberately not on this path.  Putting them first
# breaks the fork's rounds, whose stddef.h has no max_align_t where Mes's
# does; mixing the two orders differently breaks on size_t, which Mes makes
# unsigned long and tcc unsigned int.  The two header sets do not compose,
# so the library is compiled against one of them: Mes's, which is the library
# being compiled.

${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/lib
cd ${TMPDIR}

${tcc} -std=c11 -I ${incConfig} -I ${incMes} -I ${incMesArch} -c -o ${out}/lib/crt1.o ${crt1_c}
${tcc} -std=c11 -I ${incConfig} -I ${incMes} -I ${incMesArch} -c -o ${out}/lib/crti.o ${crti_c}
${tcc} -std=c11 -I ${incConfig} -I ${incMes} -I ${incMesArch} -c -o ${out}/lib/crtn.o ${crtn_c}

# libtcc1 is the COMPILER's runtime, not the C library's: the helpers tcc
# emits calls to for arithmetic the instruction set does not do in one go.
# It comes from tinycc's own lib/, beside the compiler being built -- not
# from the Mes C library, which ships a file of the same name meaning
# something else.  Taking the wrong one leaves the helpers subtly wrong: casts
# from floating point to integer quietly produce zero.
${tcc} -std=c11 -I ${incConfig} -I ${incMes} -I ${incMesArch} -D TCC_TARGET_${tccTarget}=1 ${libtccOpt1} ${libtccOpt2} -c -o libtcc1.o ${libtcc1_c}
${tcc} -std=c11 -I ${incConfig} -I ${incMes} -I ${incMesArch} -D TCC_TARGET_${tccTarget}=1 ${libtccOpt1} ${libtccOpt2} -c -o va_list.o ${valist_c}
${tcc} -ar ${out}/lib/libtcc1.a libtcc1.o va_list.o

${tcc} -std=c11 -I ${incConfig} -I ${incMes} -I ${incMesArch} -c -o libc.o ${libc_c}
${tcc} -ar ${out}/lib/libc.a libc.o

${tcc} -std=c11 -I ${incConfig} -I ${incMes} -I ${incMesArch} -c -o libgetopt.o ${getopt_c}
${tcc} -ar ${out}/lib/libgetopt.a libgetopt.o
